\title{Strange Loops: A Dependency Chain from the Liar Paradox to
Non-Vacuous G\"odelian Incompleteness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-10}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a single, self-contained dependency chain of results on self-reference,
diagonalization, and incompleteness, organized around one methodological correction. The
naive picture of a ``semantic strange loop'' --- a system whose diagonal operator returns,
for \emph{every} property, a sentence whose \emph{truth} equals that property applied to
itself --- is inconsistent: it is the Liar paradox in disguise. Genuine G\"odelian
incompleteness escapes this collapse by diagonalizing against the \emph{syntactic
provability} predicate rather than truth, and by requiring a fixed point only for the
single unprovability predicate. Starting from the propositional Liar
$\lnot(p \Leftrightarrow \lnot p)$, we (i) prove that no total semantic diagonal exists;
(ii) extract the propositional skeleton of G\"odel's First Incompleteness and
Undecidability theorems from soundness alone; (iii) present Lawvere's fixed-point theorem
as the categorical heart of every diagonal argument and derive Cantor's, Tarski's, and
Rice's theorems as corollaries; (iv) define a \emph{consistent, inhabited} provability
system and prove genuine, non-vacuous incompleteness, undecidability, and a Löb-style
unprovability of consistency; and (v) recast incompleteness order-theoretically as a
strict gap between the least and greatest fixed points of a monotone closure operator on
the lattice of theories.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Self-reference is the common engine of a diverse family of limitative results: Cantor's
theorem, Russell's paradox, Turing's halting problem, Tarski's undefinability of truth,
Rice's theorem, and G\"odel's incompleteness theorems. Each is a diagonal argument, and
each turns a would-be loop of self-reference into either a contradiction (a paradox) or a
permanent structural limitation (a theorem).

The distinction between paradox and theorem is our pivot. A \emph{paradox} arises when
self-reference loops truth directly onto truth; the system then proves its own
inconsistency. A \emph{theorem} arises when the loop is deflected through a strictly
weaker syntactic surrogate for truth --- provability --- so the loop closes at a slight
angle, leaving a residue (a true-but-unprovable sentence) instead of a contradiction. This
is Hofstadter's \emph{strange loop}: a hierarchy that, climbed far enough, folds back on
itself.

Our contribution is structural: a single dependency chain in which each result feeds the
next, culminating in an incompleteness theorem proved \textbf{non-vacuous} by an explicit
consistent model.

\section{The Liar seed}

\begin{definition}[Self-negating proposition]
A proposition $p$ is \emph{self-negating} if $p \Leftrightarrow \lnot p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Liar]\label{thm:liar}
No proposition is self-negating: $(p \Leftrightarrow \lnot p) \to \bot$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $h : p \Leftrightarrow \lnot p$. First $\lnot p$: from $p$, the forward direction
yields $\lnot p$, contradicting $p$; hence $\lnot p$. Applying the backward direction to
$\lnot p$ gives $p$, and $\lnot p$ applied to $p$ gives $\bot$.
\end{proof}

\section{The naive semantic strange loop is inconsistent}

Fix a type $S$ of sentences, a truth predicate $\mathrm{True} : S \to \Prop$, and a
diagonal operator $\mathrm{diag} : (S \to \Prop) \to S$.

\begin{definition}[Total semantic diagonal]
The pair $(\mathrm{True}, \mathrm{diag})$ is a \emph{total semantic diagonal} if for every
predicate $P : S \to \Prop$, $\ \mathrm{True}(\mathrm{diag}(P)) \Leftrightarrow
P(\mathrm{diag}(P))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[No total semantic diagonal]\label{thm:nodiag}
No total semantic diagonal exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose one exists. Instantiate $P := (s \mapsto \lnot\,\mathrm{True}(s))$; with
$d := \mathrm{diag}(P)$ the defining equivalence gives $\mathrm{True}(d) \Leftrightarrow
\lnot\,\mathrm{True}(d)$, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:liar}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Soundness correction]
Any structure demanding a total semantic diagonal is uninhabited. Genuine incompleteness
must diagonalize against a syntactic predicate weaker than truth and require the fixed
point only for a single predicate (unprovability).
\end{corollary}

\section{The abstract propositional core of G\"odel}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract incompleteness]\label{thm:absinc}
Let $P, T$ be propositions with $T \Leftrightarrow \lnot P$ and $P \to T$. Then $\lnot P$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $P$. Soundness gives $T$; then $T \Leftrightarrow \lnot P$ gives $\lnot P$, which
applied to $P$ yields $\bot$. Hence $\lnot P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Truth of the G\"odel sentence]\label{thm:abstrue}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:absinc}, $T$ holds.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:absinc}, $\lnot P$; apply the backward direction of
$T \Leftrightarrow \lnot P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Abstract undecidability]\label{thm:absundec}
Suppose additionally $T_n \Leftrightarrow \lnot T$ and $P_n \to T_n$. Then
$\lnot P \wedge \lnot P_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\lnot P$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:absinc}. For $\lnot P_n$: assume $P_n$; soundness gives
$T_n$; then $\lnot T$; but Theorem~\ref{thm:abstrue} gives $T$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{Lawvere's fixed-point theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere fixed point]\label{thm:lawvere}
Let $\varphi : A \to (A \to B)$ be point-surjective. Then every $g : B \to B$ has a fixed
point: some $b$ with $g(b) = b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply point-surjectivity to $f(a) := g(\varphi(a)(a))$, obtaining $a_0$ with
$\varphi(a_0) = f$. Evaluating at $a_0$: $\varphi(a_0)(a_0) = g(\varphi(a_0)(a_0))$, so
$b := \varphi(a_0)(a_0)$ is fixed.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cantor]\label{cor:cantor}
For any $A$ there is no surjection $f : A \to (A \to \Prop)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A surjection would, via Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere} with $g := \lnot(\cdot)$, produce
$b \Leftrightarrow \lnot b$, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:liar}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Tarski undefinability]
No surjective truth coding $\varphi : A \to (A \to \Prop)$ exists; equivalently there is a
predicate $Q$ with $\varphi(a) \neq Q$ for all $a$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Trivial property]
A property $P$ of predicates is \emph{trivial} if $(\forall a,\ P(a)) \vee
(\forall a,\ \lnot P(a))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{corollary}[Rice, abstract form]
If predicates on $A$ were exhausted by a surjection $\varphi$, every property of predicates
transported along $\varphi$ would be trivial. This holds vacuously by
Corollary~\ref{cor:cantor}.
\end{corollary}

\section{A consistent, inhabited provability system}

\begin{definition}[Provability system]\label{def:sys}
A \emph{provability system} consists of a type $\mathrm{Sentence}$; predicates
$\Prov, \Holds : \mathrm{Sentence} \to \Prop$; \textbf{soundness}
$\forall s,\ \Prov(s) \to \Holds(s)$; a negation $\Neg$ with $\Holds(\Neg\,s)
\Leftrightarrow \lnot\,\Holds(s)$; a sentence $G$; and the diagonal fixed point
$\Holds(G) \Leftrightarrow \lnot\,\Prov(G)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Non-vacuity]\label{thm:inhab}
A provability system exists.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $\mathrm{Sentence}$ the Booleans, $\Prov(s) := \bot$, $\Holds(b) := (b = \text{true})$,
$\Neg$ Boolean negation, $G := \text{true}$. Soundness is vacuous, negation commutes with
truth by cases, and the fixed point reads $\top \Leftrightarrow \top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[First Incompleteness]\label{thm:inc}
In any provability system, $\Holds(G) \wedge \lnot\,\Prov(G)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorems~\ref{thm:absinc} and \ref{thm:abstrue} with $P := \Prov(G)$,
$T := \Holds(G)$, using the diagonal fixed point and soundness at $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Incompleteness]
$\exists s,\ \Holds(s) \wedge \lnot\,\Prov(s)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[No completeness]
$\lnot\,\forall s,\ \Holds(s) \to \Prov(s)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Undecidability of $G$]
$\lnot\,\Prov(G) \wedge \lnot\,\Prov(\Neg\,G)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:absundec} with the diagonal fixed point, soundness at $G$, the
negation law, and soundness at $\Neg\,G$.
\end{proof}

\section{A second-incompleteness / L\"ob-style corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency is unprovable]\label{thm:loeb}
Let a provability system contain $\Con$ with $\Holds(\Con) \Leftrightarrow \lnot\,\Prov(G)$
and satisfy the derivability condition $\Prov(\Con) \to \Prov(G)$. Then
$\lnot\,\Prov(\Con)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Assume $\Prov(\Con)$. Soundness gives $\Holds(\Con)$, so $\lnot\,\Prov(G)$; but the
derivability condition gives $\Prov(G)$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\section{The provability lattice}

Model a theory as an element of a complete lattice $\alpha$ and ``close under inference'' as
a monotone operator $f : \alpha \to \alpha$; fixed points are the deductively closed
theories.

\begin{theorem}[Knaster--Tarski]\label{thm:kt}
Every monotone $f : \alpha \to \alpha$ on a complete lattice has a fixed point; in
particular a least $\lfp(f)$ and a greatest $\gfp(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Minimality]
For any pre-fixed point $a$ (i.e.\ $f(a) \le a$), $\lfp(f) \le a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Gap forces incompleteness]
If $\lfp(f) \neq \gfp(f)$, then $\lfp(f) < \gfp(f)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Always $\lfp(f) \le \gfp(f)$; combine with the inequality.
\end{proof}

The strict inequality realizes incompleteness as the width of a gap: sentences in the
maximal consistent extension but absent from the provable core.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

Self-reference is survivable only when tangled across levels that may disagree: loop truth
onto truth and one gets the Liar; loop truth onto provability and the loop leaves a
true-but-unprovable residue. Non-vacuity (Theorem~\ref{thm:inhab}) guarantees the results
concern real objects. Natural continuations: (1) construct the diagonal lemma from
arithmetized syntax rather than assuming it; (2) formalize the Hilbert--Bernays--L\"ob
conditions and L\"ob's theorem $\Box(\Box A \to A) \to \Box A$ (modal logic GL);
(3) weaken soundness to simple consistency via Rosser sentences; (4) study randomized
provability operators and Chaitin's $\Omega$ as a measure-theoretic face of incompleteness;
(5) formalize multi-level self-referential hierarchies.

